# Prompts tokenized with Python's lock released. The Rust tokenizer's single-text encode holds the GIL for the whole
# text, so while one request's prompt is tokenized no other thread of the engine process runs, the decode loop of every
# other request included: on two GB10s a 1,019,111-token request paused another request's stream for about 2 s, and a
# /tokenize call does the same. The tokenizer's batch calls release the GIL while Rust tokenizes and return the same ids;
# every prompt tokenization now goes through encode_batch_fast (encode_batch before tokenizers 0.20), which skips the
# character offsets prompts never use (tensorfold/cuda/unlocked.py: chat and completion prompts, /tokenize, the media
# markers' template, a picture request's layout in vision/glm.py, and 9005's new part). Measured on a GB10 core,
# preparing a 996,815-token chat request (render, tokenize, 9004/9005): every other thread was held off for up to
# 2,211 ms before, 73 ms after, and the preparation took 2.0 s instead of 2.6 s; laying out a ~400K-token picture
# request held them off 1,066 ms before (x86 workstation). mgllm, 2026-10-08.
# Tests: tests/test_9006_unlocked_tokenize.py (CPU; needs TF_GLM_TEST_MODEL_DIR).

